Patch against the `wingleeio/zed` gpui fork, pinned rev
e2ddcc6805f8c5088e62a60dfe517abcccd61a9a (see workspace `Cargo.toml` and
`AGENTS.md`'s "The GPUI dependency decision"). Applied by
`scripts/setup-gpui-patch.sh` — see that script and `AGENTS.md`'s "Summon
latency" section for the full diagnosis and why this specific change is
correct.

Root cause: `crates/gpui/src/window.rs`'s `on_request_frame` handler caps
frame delivery to a window to ~30fps whenever the window isn't key
(`!active.get()`), with no exemption for a pending `on_next_frame`/
`request_animation_frame` callback — something a caller is *explicitly*
waiting on, not an idle background redraw. This throttle does not exist at
all in the published `gpui = "0.2.2"` crate. neko's summon panel is a
non-activating `orderFrontRegardless` show (real summon also races the
async `windowDidBecomeKey:` notification against its own first frame
request), so it is essentially always "inactive" from this check's point of
view at the moment its first post-summon frame is requested — every such
frame was capped to the 30fps floor, and because a throttled frame never
advances `last_frame_time`, every subsequent request landed in the same
stale window and got throttled again, locking summon into a ~25-40ms
cadence with no way out. This patch exempts a frame request carrying a
pending callback from the inactive-window cap specifically, leaving thermal
throttling (which protects the machine regardless of what's being waited
on) untouched.

Trade-off, accepted deliberately: a hypothetical future *repeating*
animation (`request_animation_frame` called again from within its own
callback) in a window that stays inactive/unfocused would no longer be
power-throttled to 30fps either, since its callback queue is never
genuinely empty. neko's own animation catalog (`crates/neko/src/motion.rs`)
is exclusively one-shot as of this patch — see `AGENTS.md`'s "Comet craft
pass" — so this does not regress anything today. If a repeating animation
is ever added, revisit this patch (a "was this window ever active while
this callback was pending" flag would be the more surgical fix, at the cost
of new cross-platform `Platform`/`PlatformWindow` plumbing this patch
avoids).

diff --git a/crates/gpui/src/window.rs b/crates/gpui/src/window.rs
--- a/crates/gpui/src/window.rs
+++ b/crates/gpui/src/window.rs
@@ -1516,16 +1516,35 @@

                 // Throttle frame rate based on conditions:
                 // - Thermal pressure (Serious/Critical): cap to ~60fps
-                // - Inactive window (not focused): cap to ~30fps to save energy
+                // - Inactive window (not focused) with nothing pending: cap to
+                //   ~30fps to save energy
+                //
+                // The inactive-window cap must not delay a frame that
+                // something is actually waiting on. A non-empty
+                // `next_frame_callbacks` means a caller explicitly asked to
+                // be told when the next frame lands (`Window::on_next_frame`/
+                // `request_animation_frame`) — not an idle background
+                // redraw. Applying the 30fps floor to it anyway means any
+                // window that is shown without also being made key first
+                // (e.g. `orderFrontRegardless`, or the brief window between
+                // a real activate call and the async `windowDidBecomeKey:`
+                // notification actually landing) has its very first paint
+                // — and every one after it, since a throttled frame never
+                // advances `last_frame_time` and the next request lands in
+                // the same stale window — locked to a ~33ms cadence with no
+                // way out. Exempting pending callbacks fixes that without
+                // touching thermal throttling, which still protects the
+                // machine regardless of what's being waited on.
+                let has_pending_callback = !next_frame_callbacks.borrow().is_empty();
                 let min_frame_interval = if !request_frame_options.force_render
                     && !request_frame_options.require_presentation
-                    && next_frame_callbacks.borrow().is_empty()
+                    && !has_pending_callback
                 {
                     None
-                } else if !active.get() {
-                    Some(Duration::from_micros(33333))
                 } else if let Some(ThermalState::Critical | ThermalState::Serious) = thermal_state {
                     Some(Duration::from_micros(16667))
+                } else if !active.get() && !has_pending_callback {
+                    Some(Duration::from_micros(33333))
                 } else {
                     None
                 };
